---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="About CappingChucks.com: Editorial Policy and Sources"
    description="What CappingChucks.com covers, how the guides are researched and fact-checked, how to read the numbers, and how to send a correction."
    canonical="https://cappingchucks.com/about/"
    breadcrumb="About"
>
    <PageHero title="About This Guide" crumb="About" answerLabel="What this site is">
        <p>CappingChucks.com is a plain-English reference on capping chucks, capping headsets, cap torque, and cap sizes. It does not sell parts, give quotes, or service machines.</p>
    </PageHero>

    <main>
        <section id="sources" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>How the guides are prepared</h2>
                <p>Technical claims cite their source next to the text they support. We prefer standards groups such as ASTM, ISBT, and CETIE. We also use regulations (eCFR, EUR-Lex) and documents from capper, headset, and cap makers. Distributor charts, like the <a href="/cap-torque/#chart">cap torque chart</a>, are labeled as guides, not specs.</p>
                <p>A maker's figure describes that maker's product. We say whose number it is instead of applying it to every machine. Where no source gives a number, we leave it out and point you to your cap maker or machine maker.</p>
                <p>We don't claim hands-on machine testing or professional reviewer credentials. We don't publish "best" rankings.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="review" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>Fact-checking and review status</h2>
                <p>In October 2026, every page was checked against its cited sources. Claims that didn't hold up were corrected or removed. For example, we corrected how magnetic headsets are adjusted and how far removal torque drops after capping.</p>
                <p>A named packaging engineer has not reviewed the guides yet. When one does, we will credit them here.</p>
                <p>Always confirm settings against your cap maker's spec, your machine manual, and your own quality plan before changing a line.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="disclosures" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>Commercial disclosures</h2>
                <p>This site has no affiliate links, paid placements, sponsored rankings, or lead sales. Links to makers are references, not endorsements. Brand names belong to their owners and are used only to identify machines and parts. If we ever add a business tie that could change how you read a page, we will disclose it on that page.</p>
                <p>Photos come from <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Pexels</a> under its free license. Diagrams are our own.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="corrections" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>Send a correction</h2>
                <p>Found a mistake? Use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Name the page and the exact claim, and link to a standard, manual, or maker document that supports the fix.</p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
